# Pontif alt syntax — canonical reference
#
# ============================================================================
# LOAD-BEARING PRINCIPLES
# ============================================================================
#
# 1. BRACKETS WRAP SORTS.  `[...]` is the universal sort delimiter.
#    A bare identifier in sort position is sugar for `[Ident]`:
#         Int   ≡   [Int]
#
# 2. COLON NARROWS.  `:` is the universal "narrows to" operator:
#         x:Int                       # param x narrows to Int
#         [Int:@>0]                   # Int narrows to values where @>0
#         [Point(x:[Int:0])]          # Point's x narrows to (Int equal to 0)
#         function f():Ret            # f narrows to producing Ret
#    RHS of `:` is always a SORT, never a value.  Singleton constraints
#    use `[Sort:value]` (e.g., `x:[Int:0]`, not `x:0`).
#
# 3. @ IS THE PRINCIPAL SUBJECT of the enclosing refinement:
#         - Refinement [Sort:pred]:  the value being refined
#         - Match arm:               the scrutinee
#    Each refinement binds its own `@`.  Nested refinements SHADOW the
#    outer `@`.  Methods get `this` injected as the receiver (so the
#    receiver has a stable name independent of `@`).
#
# 4. `|` IS OR, `&` IS AND, `!` IS NOT, AT EVERY LEVEL.  The level is
#    determined by where in the bracket form the operator sits:
#
#         - SORT LEVEL — inside [...] but not after a `:`:
#               [Int|Float]                 # union of bare sorts
#               [Even & Positive]           # intersection
#               [[Int:0]|[Float:@>1]]       # union of refined sorts
#               [Int|[Float:@>1]]           # mixed bare + refined
#
#         - PREDICATE LEVEL — inside [Base:...]:
#               [Int:@<0 | @>10]            # predicate-level OR
#               [Int:@>0 & @<10]            # predicate-level AND
#               [Int:!(@>0)]                # predicate-level NOT
#
#    Once `:` is consumed inside a bracket form, the rest is predicate
#    until the matching `]`.  To get a sort-level union of refined sorts,
#    each refined sort is its own sub-bracket: `[[Int:0]|[Int:1]]`.
#
#    NORMALIZATION: same-base sort-level unions / intersections collapse
#    at parse time to a single refined sort with merged predicate, so
#    there's one canonical IR shape per refinement equivalence class:
#         [[Int:0]|[Int:1]]      ≡   [Int:0|1]
#         [[Int:@>0]&[Int:@<10]] ≡   [Int:@>0 & @<10]
#    Cross-base unions / intersections stay as sort-level Union /
#    Intersection in the IR (no shared base to merge under).  Origins
#    are preserved: the outer Refined gets a span across the whole
#    source form; each sub-predicate keeps its parse-time origin so
#    error messages still point at the offending disjunct.
#
#    The single-character forms are LOGICAL.  Bitwise operators do not
#    exist in Pontif — this is a math language, not a systems language.
#
# 5. IMPLICIT `@==EXPR` SUGAR.  When a refinement predicate is a plain
#    expression with no top-level comparison, an implicit `@==` is
#    inserted.  Applied per-disjunct/conjunct for `|`/`&` chains:
#         [Int:n*2]      ≡   [Int:@==n*2]
#         [Bool:true]    ≡   [Bool:@==true]
#         [Int:0|1]      ≡   [Int:@==0 | @==1]
#
# 6. BASE-INFERENCE.  When ONE base sort is unambiguous from context,
#    the base may be omitted:
#         match (x:Int) { [@<0] -> ... }   # base inferred = Int
#    Required to be explicit when context is ambiguous (union scrutinee,
#    top-level function return, etc.):
#         function foo():[Int:@>0]         # required, not [@>0]
#
# 7. `.{ ... }` IS NAMED DECOMPOSITION — one payload, three consumers.
#    Brackets are reserved for sorts; `.{}` reads "from X, take {these}".
#    Imports, exports, and by-name value destructure are the SAME operation:
#         requires math.{min, max, avg, floor}
#         exports  @.{factorial, isEven, isOdd, sign}
#         let person.{name, age}               # binds name, age from person
#    An entry renames inline with `->` ("becomes" — the same arrow as match
#    arms and function bodies). LHS = the name where the symbol already
#    lives; RHS = its name in the receiving context:
#         requires math.{min -> minimum, max}
#         let person.{name -> username, age}
#    (Exports rename is parked — public!=internal naming needs an export-side
#    mapping; the parser rejects it with a pointer.)
#    By-name reads are PROJECTIONS: taking a subset is honest (unlike
#    positional patterns, which must be arity-total — naming a key claims
#    only "there is one"). An unknown key is a lie and is rejected when the
#    source's sort is statically known. Tuples are excluded: positional
#    components are destructure-only.
#
# 8. MATCH IS TOTAL.  Every match must cover every possible value of the
#    scrutinee's sort — no undefined behavior.  The compiler proves the
#    union of arm predicates equals the scrutinee's sort, or the program
#    fails to compile.  A non-exhaustive match would betray Pontif's
#    whole identity (proof at compile time, not runtime).
#
#    Arms are evaluated TOP-TO-BOTTOM.  The first arm whose predicate
#    matches the scrutinee wins.  Overlap is allowed: write the specific
#    case first, the general case below.  Later arms covering values
#    already handled by earlier arms are dead code (allowed; the
#    compiler may warn).
#
#    The `_` default arm is the ergonomic catch-all.  It desugars at
#    parse time to the explicit complement of the other arms' union
#    over the scrutinee's sort:
#         match x { [@<0] -> A; _ -> B }
#       ≡ match x { [@<0] -> A; [@>=0] -> B }
#    Same totality guarantee either way — the IR only ever sees explicit
#    predicates.  `_` must be the last arm; only one `_` per match.
#
#    DISPATCH IS DIFFERENT.  Function-overload dispatch is *unordered*:
#    the compiler verifies pairwise at registration time that no two
#    overloads' param patterns can match the same value (non-overlapping
#    overloads).  Resolution is then a function of the argument value
#    alone, independent of declaration order.
#
# ============================================================================

module alt.syntax

requires math.{min, max, avg, floor}
exports  @.{factorial, isEven, isOdd, sign}

# === Pure functions + overloading ===========================================

function factorial(n:[Int:0])  :Int -> 1
function factorial(n:[Int:@>0]):Int -> n * factorial(n-1)

# Dispatch enables mutual recursion.  Return sort `[Int:0|1]` is two
# singleton refinements OR'd together (per-disjunct @== sugar).

function isEven(n:[Int:0])  :[Int:0|1] -> 1
function isEven(n:[Int:@>0]):[Int:0|1] -> isOdd(n-1)
function isOdd (n:[Int:0])  :[Int:0|1] -> 0
function isOdd (n:[Int:@>0]):[Int:0|1] -> isEven(n-1)

# === Pattern matching =======================================================

# Inside `match SCRUTINEE`, an arm's @ is the scrutinee.  When the
# scrutinee has a single unambiguous base, the base may be omitted.

function sign(n:Int):[Int:-1|0|1] -> match n
  [@<0 ] -> -1
  [@==0] ->  0
  [@>0 ] ->  1

# === Provability ============================================================

# Body must produce a value that satisfies the return refinement.
function squareNonneg(x:[Int:@>=0]):[Int:@>=0] -> x*x

# === Spec-only declarations =================================================
# No body needed when the return sort pins a single value.

function alwaysTrue() :[Bool:true]
function alwaysFalse():[Bool:false]

# Return type parameterized by argument — value fully determined.
function timesTwo(n:Int):[Int:n*2]

# === Method sorts ===========================================================
# Method sorts use `Method(params):return`, wrapped in brackets like
# any other sort.  Params may be named (for dependent returns) or
# anonymous (positional).

# Named params — return sort references the input.
function addN(n:Int):[Method(x:Int):[Int:x+n]]

# Anonymous params — used when return doesn't depend on inputs.
function applyTwice(f:[Method(Int):Int], x:Int):Int -> f(f(x))

# === Structs ================================================================

# Declaration introduces a name; uses wrap in brackets.
struct Point(x:Int, y:Int)

# Construction.  Two surface forms, both produce the same record value:
#   - Positional `Point(a, b)`               — args in declared field order.
#   - By-name    `Point{x=a, y=b}`           — order-free; canonicalized to
#                                              declared order in the IR.
# `=` is the field/value separator — it's value-level assignment, distinct
# from `:` (narrows-to).  The brace form is the general "by-name args"
# syntax: anywhere positional `name(args)` is legal, `name{field=val,...}`
# will be too (function calls and match-patterns are future slices; in this
# slice it's struct constructors only).
function makePoint(a:Int, b:Int):Point -> Point(a, b)
function shiftedPoint(p:Point):Point -> Point{x=p.x+1, y=p.y+1}

# Static value via direct construction.  Sort is inferred maximally-specific
# from the value (`origin` ends up with sort [Point(x:[Int:@==0], y:[Int:@==0])])
# so dispatch has the tightest narrowing.  Bare references to `origin` in
# expression position rewrite to a 0-arg call so users don't have to write
# `origin()` everywhere.  Dotted let names work the same way:
# `Point.origin.x` becomes `Call("Point.origin", []).x`.
let origin = Point(0, 0)
let Point.origin = Point(0, 0)        # `Point.origin` and `Point.origin.x` both work

# Lets and 0-arg functions are interchangeable at use sites.  Both are 0-arg
# dispatch entries; both auto-Call on bare reference (qualified or not).
# Functions with at least one param require explicit parens at the call site.
function Point.zero():Point -> Point(0, 0)
function five():Int -> 5
let twelve = Point.zero.x + Point.zero.y + 12 + five
let n = 5                                # n : [Int:@==5]
let b = true                             # b : [Bool:@==true]
let z = n + 1                            # z : [Int:@==(n() + 1)]

# An explicit sort annotation acts as a base-name sanity check; the binding's
# effective sort is still the value's maximally-specific inferred sort.
let origin2:Point = Point(0, 0)

# In-expression let: same surface form, lives inside a function body or any
# other expression context.  The body of each let is the rest of the
# enclosing expression — wrap multi-let chains in `{ ... }` to give them a
# clean closing boundary.  Block braces are a pure delimiter (no IR-level
# meaning; the block unwraps to its inner expression).
function shifted(p:Point):Point ->
{
  let dx = p.x + 1
  let dy = p.y + 1
  Point(dx, dy)
}

# Single-let bodies don't need the wrapper:
function plusOne(n:Int):Int -> let m = n + 1 m * 2

# Static value, maximally specified — value synthesized from the sort.
# This form still emits NoOp pending the proof engine's synthesis pipeline.
let Point.staticOrigin:[Point(x:[Int:0], y:[Int:0])]

# Static function — `@` in the return refinement is the produced Int.
function Point.manhattan(p:Point):[Int:p.x+p.y]

# Instance method.  `this` is the receiver (injected by method desugar).
# `@` inside each field constraint is that field's value-under-refinement
# (an Int here), so we use `this.x` / `this.y` to reach receiver fields.
# The form below is spec-only — it pins the return refinement but needs the
# proof engine to synthesize the body.  Methods with explicit bodies (next
# line) work at runtime today.
method Point.add(p:Point):[Point(x:[Int:this.x+p.x], y:[Int:this.y+p.y])]

# Method with an explicit body — callable from instance values via
# `receiver.add(other)`, which the parser routes to `Point.add(receiver,
# other)` when it knows the receiver is a Point.
method Point.plus(p:Point):Point -> Point(this.x + p.x, this.y + p.y)
function combine(p:Point, q:Point):Point -> p.plus(q)
let shifted = origin.plus(Point(1, 2))   # works on a let-bound value too

# Operator overloading.  Operators are symmetric multi-dispatch FREE FUNCTIONS
# — declared `function <op>(a:T, b:T):R`, dispatched by matching BOTH operand
# types (exactly like every other function).  An infix `a <op> b` always parses
# to a primitive BinOp; the post-link resolver routes it to the matching
# overload by operand sort, leaving built-in Int/Bool ops as BinOp.  The
# receiver-rooted `method Type.<op>` form is REJECTED — it forces the receiver
# to be the first operand, the asymmetry symmetric dispatch removes.
# Refinement-predicate arithmetic stays primitive.  Overloadable: + - * / % ^
# < <= > >= == !=
function +(a:Point, b:Point):Point -> Point(a.x + b.x, a.y + b.y)
function ==(a:Point, b:Point):Bool -> a.x == b.x & a.y == b.y
function shift(a:Point, b:Point):Point -> a + b
function same(a:Point, b:Point):Bool -> a == b

# === Traits =================================================================
# A trait is a sort that describes a method contract.  Types opt in via
# `assign trait`; satisfaction is checked at narrowing time by SortChecker
# and dispatch is resolved at runtime by consulting the TraitRegistry.
# Narrowing handles polymorphism — no parallel trait-dispatch mechanism.
let Duck:Type{
  quack:[Method():Int]
}

assign trait Point:Duck {
  quack():Int -> this.x + this.y
}

# A trait-typed parameter: any value whose concrete type satisfies Duck
# is accepted.  Inside the body, `d.quack()` routes through the dispatch
# table's trait-fallback to the concrete `Point.quack` (or any other
# registered Duck implementor).
function describe(d:Duck):Int -> d.quack()
describe(Point(3, 4))   # → 7

# Match destructure — bare idents in clause positions BIND.  A clause
# like `field:[Sort:pred]` constrains the field's sort instead of binding.
# Mixing is allowed.
function nested(p:Point):Int -> match p {
  [Point(x, y)] -> match x {
    [@<0 ] -> -y
    [@>=0] ->  y
  }
}

# ============================================================================
# REFERENCE — all bracket forms at a glance
# ============================================================================
#
#   [Int]                              # base sort  (= Int, bare-ident sugar)
#   [Int|Float|Bool]                   # union of bare sorts
#   [Even & Positive]                  # intersection (refinements of one base)
#   [Int:@>0]                          # refined, explicit predicate
#   [Int:@>0 & @<10]                   # refined, AND
#   [Int:@==0 | @==1 | @==2]           # refined, OR
#   [Int:!(@>0)]                       # refined, NOT  (non-positive Ints)
#   [Int:0|1|2]                        # same, via per-disjunct @== sugar
#   [Int:42]                           # singleton  (= [Int:@==42])
#   [Bool:true]  /  [Bool:false]       # bool singletons
#   [[Int:0]|[Float:@>1]]              # union of refined sorts (cross-base)
#   [[Int:0]|[Int:1]] ≡ [Int:0|1]      # union of refined sorts (same-base, normalized)
#   [Int|[Float:@>1]]                  # mixed bare + refined union
#   [Point(x:Int, y:Int)]              # structural sort
#   [Point(x:[Int:0], y:[Int:0])]      # struct with value constraints
#   [Point(x:[Int:@>0])]               # struct with refined field
#   [Point(x, y)]                      # match destructure (binds x, y)
#   [Point(x:[Int:@>0], y)]            # mixed: constrain x, bind y
#   [Method(Int, Int):Int]             # method sort, anonymous params
#   [Method(x:Int):[Int:x+1]]          # method sort, named (dependent)
#   [(Int, Bool)]                      # tuple sort — anonymous positional aggregate
#   [([Int:@>0], Bool)]                # tuple with a refined component
#   [(a, b)]                           # tuple destructure (binds a, b)
#   [(a, _, c)]                        # tuple destructure with a `_` discard
#
# Value-level (struct construction) — NOT in brackets:
#   Point(1, 2)                        # positional construction
#   Point{x=1, y=2}                    # by-name construction
#   Point{y=2, x=1}                    # by-name, free order (canonicalized)
#   (1, true)                          # tuple — anonymous positional aggregate (arity >= 2)
#   {a = 1, b = true}                  # dictionary — anonymous by-name aggregate
#   let d.{a, b -> bee}                # by-name decomposition: binds a, bee from d
#
# A positional `(...)` pattern is arity-total — it must account for every slot
# (`_` discards one). Subset patterns like [Point(x)] are rejected (lying by
# omission); focus on one field by name with a refinement [Point:@.x>0] instead.
# Tuple components are destructure-only: no value-level `t._0` (only `@._0`
# inside a refinement predicate).
#
# THE CLAIM RULE — construction makes claims, matching tests them:
#   let p:Point = {x = 1, y = 2}        # OK — the annotation asserts Point;
#                                       # checked construction, name elided
#   f({x = 1, y = 2})                   # OK when f's param is Point-typed
#   f(Vec(1, 2))                        # REJECTED when f wants Point — a Vec's
#                                       # claim is never silently re-badged
#   match d { [Point] -> ... }          # a QUESTION: an anonymous d answers no;
#                                       # only constructed Points answer yes
#   Point(1, 2) == {x = 1, y = 2}       # false — equality follows matching;
#                                       # coerce earlier (let / typed argument)
#
# Block expression — pure delimiter, useful for multi-let bodies:
#   { let a = 1 let b = 2 a + b }      # evaluates to 3
